00:17:06,690 --> 00:17:11,170
It could also help us identify areas where we can build resilience, especially in how

234
00:17:11,170 --> 00:17:14,930
we forecast demand and manage supplier relationships.

235
00:17:14,930 --> 00:17:16,370
That makes sense.

236
00:17:16,370 --> 00:17:21,330
When we consider the complexity of today's supply chains, global sourcing, fluctuating

237
00:17:21,330 --> 00:17:27,170
demand, compliance requirements, taking a step back to analyze the full picture is not only

238
00:17:27,170 --> 00:17:29,490
practical but necessary.

239
00:17:29,490 --> 00:17:35,250
So perhaps we can start by revisiting our demand forecasting and inventory planning

240
00:17:35,250 --> 00:17:42,090
and then proceed to supplier evaluation, negotiations, logistics, and risk mitigation.

241
00:17:42,090 --> 00:17:43,680
Exactly.

242
00:17:43,680 --> 00:17:46,960
Let me start with demand forecasting.

243
00:17:46,960 --> 00:17:52,160
Over the past three quarters, our variance has been significantly higher than typical.

244
00:17:52,160 --> 00:17:58,160
We've usually kept forecasts within a 7% deviation, but recently we've seen swings upward

245
00:17:58,160 --> 00:18:00,160
of 18%.

246
00:18:00,160 --> 00:18:05,240
Such volatility affects our procurement planning, cash flow, and even the reliability of our

247
00:18:05,240 --> 00:18:07,200
commitments to key clients.

248
00:18:07,200 --> 00:18:08,200
You're right.

249
00:18:08,200 --> 00:18:13,640
I reviewed some of the data from the last quarter, and I noticed the same.

250
00:18:13,640 --> 00:18:20,480
A major issue seems to be that our forecasting model heavily relies on historical sales without

251
00:18:20,480 --> 00:18:26,720
giving enough weight to external indicators, market trends, macroeconomic shifts, competitor

252
00:18:26,720 --> 00:18:30,680
behavior, and even geopolitical influences.

253
00:18:30,680 --> 00:18:35,640
Our forecasting team is talented, but the tools they use may not fully capture the

254
00:18:35,640 --> 00:18:38,120
dynamics of the current business landscape.

255
00:18:38,120 --> 00:18:39,120
True.

256
00:18:39,120 --> 00:18:44,120
The model served us well when demand patterns were stable, but today's environment demands

257
00:18:44,120 --> 00:18:46,320
more adaptive systems.

258
00:18:46,320 --> 00:18:51,960
I've been thinking that we should integrate more predictive analytics, AI-driven models,

259
00:18:51,960 --> 00:18:55,720
scenario planning, even real-time demand sensing.

260
00:18:55,720 --> 00:18:57,560
That could help us reduce the variance.

261
00:18:57,560 --> 00:18:59,230
I agree.

262
00:18:59,230 --> 00:19:05,110
Another factor is the communication flow between sales, product development, operations

263
00:19:05,110 --> 00:19:10,630
and procurement. Sometimes the sales team receives early signals about customer shifts,

264
00:19:10,630 --> 00:19:16,170
but doesn't relay them fast enough. By the time procurement adjusts the orders, suppliers

265
00:19:16,170 --> 00:19:21,670
have limited capacity or extended lead times, which creates stress throughout the chain.

266
00:19:21,670 --> 00:19:27,390
Exactly. The classic information lag. If we could strengthen our sales and operations

267
00:19:27,390 --> 00:19:33,030
planning collaboration, we'd be able to align demand with supply more accurately.

268
00:19:33,030 --> 00:19:38,730
monthly alignment meetings is good, but maybe we should increase the frequency or implement

269
00:19:38,730 --> 00:19:42,410
a rolling SNOP cycle with more dynamic adjustments.

270
00:19:42,410 --> 00:19:44,920
A rolling model could help.

271
00:19:44,920 --> 00:19:49,720
Now, shifting to procurement practices, I've noticed that some of our supplier contracts

272
00:19:49,720 --> 00:19:52,760
haven't been updated in nearly three years.

273
00:19:52,760 --> 00:19:57,720
Certain clauses, especially regarding price adjustment, delivery flexibility, and risk

274
00:19:57,720 --> 00:20:01,340
allocation, don't reflect current realities.

275
00:20:01,340 --> 00:20:05,620
Suppliers are asking for cost escalations, but we don't have the framework to negotiate

276
00:20:05,620 --> 00:20:08,220
fairly or push back when necessary.

277
00:20:08,220 --> 00:20:10,940
Yes, that's been a challenge.

278
00:20:10,940 --> 00:20:16,220
Many suppliers have informal agreements with us, and while that used to be fine, it's

279
00:20:16,220 --> 00:20:18,060
no longer sustainable.

280
00:20:18,060 --> 00:20:24,340
We need more structured, well-defined contracts that include SLAs, price review mechanisms,

281
00:20:24,340 --> 00:20:27,420
performance metrics, and risk-sharing models.

282
00:20:27,420 --> 00:20:30,580
A clear contract protects both sides.

283
00:20:30,580 --> 00:20:36,500
Exactly. I also think we should revisit our supplier segmentation. Right now, we have a

284
00:20:36,500 --> 00:20:42,500
fairly broad classification, critical suppliers, strategic suppliers, non-critical suppliers,

285
00:20:42,500 --> 00:20:48,160
and spot suppliers. But the way they're categorized doesn't necessarily reflect their actual

286
00:20:48,160 --> 00:20:52,500
impact on production, risk exposure, or financial value.

287
00:20:52,500 --> 00:20:58,140
I've been thinking the same. For example, some suppliers classified as non-critical

288
00:20:58,140 --> 00:21:03,180
actually supply components that could halt production if disrupted. On the other hand,

289
00:21:03,180 --> 00:21:08,940
some suppliers considered strategic may not contribute significantly to competitive advantage

290
00:21:08,940 --> 00:21:14,460
or innovation. So maybe we need a more refined segmentation model, something like the

291
00:21:14,460 --> 00:21:20,220
crawl check matrix where suppliers are evaluated on profit impact and supply risk.

292
00:21:20,220 --> 00:21:26,700
That's a good approach. If we categorize suppliers using that matrix, leverage items,

293
00:21:26,700 --> 00:21:33,660
strategic items, routine items, and bottleneck items, we can craft more tailored strategies.

294
00:21:33,660 --> 00:21:39,180
For instance, strategic items require stronger partnerships and long-term agreements,

295
00:21:39,180 --> 00:21:44,300
while routine items could be handled through automation or consolidated purchasing.

296
00:21:44,300 --> 00:21:50,780
Right. And for bottleneck items we need contingency plans, multiple sources, inventory buffers,

297
00:21:50,780 --> 00:21:54,100
or redesigning components to reduce dependency.

298
00:21:54,100 --> 00:21:57,060
The goal is resilience and agility.

299
00:21:57,060 --> 00:22:00,540
Speaking of resilience, let's talk about supplier.

300
00:22:00,540 --> 00:22:01,900
Good morning.

301
00:22:01,900 --> 00:22:04,540
Thanks for making the time to sit down today.

302
00:22:04,540 --> 00:22:06,460
I know your schedule has been packed

303
00:22:06,460 --> 00:22:08,380
and with the quarter closing,

304
00:22:08,380 --> 00:22:10,620
procurement topics have been piling up

305
00:22:10,620 --> 00:22:12,540
faster than we expected.

306
00:22:12,540 --> 00:22:14,140
But I thought it would be useful

307
00:22:14,140 --> 00:22:17,020
if we could walk through a full overview

308
00:22:17,020 --> 00:22:19,140
of the supply chain performance issues

309
00:22:19,140 --> 00:22:20,780
we've been observing.

310
00:22:20,780 --> 00:22:26,100
I'd like us to get aligned, especially because leadership is starting to request more frequent

311
00:22:26,100 --> 00:22:27,100
updates.

312
00:22:27,100 --> 00:22:28,940
So, let's dive in.

313
00:22:28,940 --> 00:22:29,940
How does that sound?

314
00:22:29,940 --> 00:22:30,940
Absolutely.

315
00:22:30,940 --> 00:22:33,180
I'm glad we're doing this.

316
00:22:33,180 --> 00:22:38,700
It feels like we've had bits and pieces of this conversation across different emails

317
00:22:38,700 --> 00:22:44,620
and short meetings, but not a full comprehensive discussion.

318
00:22:44,620 --> 00:22:51,060
And with what's happening globally, the volatility and raw material availability, freight cost

319
00:22:51,060 --> 00:22:57,300
spikes, and the ongoing supplier reliability challenges, we definitely need a consolidated

320
00:22:57,300 --> 00:22:58,300
view.

321
00:22:58,300 --> 00:23:00,420
So yes, this is the right time.

322
00:23:00,420 --> 00:23:02,420
Where would you like to begin?

323
00:23:02,420 --> 00:23:06,780
Maybe we start with the most pressing point, supplier performance.

324
00:23:06,780 --> 00:23:13,660
Over the past three months, we've seen on time delivery drop from 87% down to 73%.

325
00:23:13,660 --> 00:23:17,780
And as you know, that's caused a ripple effect in production planning.

326
00:23:17,780 --> 00:23:22,060
Our manufacturing teams have had to issue multiple schedule adjustments.

327
00:23:22,060 --> 00:23:27,940
They're frustrated, our customers feel the delays, and it's becoming a reputational risk.

328
00:23:27,940 --> 00:23:29,300
What's your perspective?

329
00:23:29,300 --> 00:23:32,660
Have suppliers provided any credible explanations?

330
00:23:32,660 --> 00:23:37,860
I've spoken with several of them, and honestly, the reasons are mixed.

331
00:23:37,860 --> 00:23:44,120
Some cite labor shortages, some point to port congestion, and others blame upstream shortages

332
00:23:44,120 --> 00:23:46,240
from their own suppliers.

333
00:23:46,240 --> 00:23:52,860
But what concerns me is that many of these explanations feel reactive, rather than strategic.

334
00:23:52,860 --> 00:23:56,980
It's like they're trying to put out fires instead of preventing them.

335
00:23:56,980 --> 00:24:02,460
And that tells me we may need to evaluate whether certain suppliers are still the right

336
00:24:02,460 --> 00:24:04,380
long-term partners.

337
00:24:04,380 --> 00:24:10,580
I agree, but before we get to that decision point, I think it's important to analyze whether

338
00:24:10,580 --> 00:24:16,580
the performance issues are due to their internal inefficiencies, or if there's something

339
00:24:16,580 --> 00:24:21,260
systemic in our processes that's contributing to the delays.

340
00:24:21,260 --> 00:24:24,820
For instance, our forecasting hasn't exactly been stable.

341
00:24:24,820 --> 00:24:29,900
We've had several last-minute quantity adjustments, and although they weren't huge changes,

342
00:24:29,900 --> 00:24:32,900
some suppliers are sensitive to fluctuations.

343
00:24:32,900 --> 00:24:36,770
Do you think our forecasting accuracy is part of the problem?

344
00:24:36,770 --> 00:24:40,550
It could be, but only partially.

345
00:24:40,550 --> 00:24:49,570
Forecasting accuracy dropped from around 80% to 72%, which is noticeable, but not catastrophic.

346
00:24:49,570 --> 00:24:53,190
Many mature suppliers can handle that level of variation.

347
00:24:53,190 --> 00:24:57,810
The bigger issue, from what I've observed, is that some of our supply partners are

348
00:24:57,810 --> 00:25:01,250
still operating with outdated planning systems.

349
00:25:01,250 --> 00:25:07,370
They rely heavily on manual entry, spreadsheets, and internal communication chains that are

350
00:25:07,370 --> 00:25:10,500
slow.

351
00:25:10,500 --> 00:25:16,660
And when they're slow communication, even small forecasting changes can cause major disruptions.

352
00:25:16,660 --> 00:25:17,660
Right.

353
00:25:17,660 --> 00:25:22,580
I've also noticed that their response times to our inquiries have increased.

354
00:25:22,580 --> 00:25:29,060
What used to be a 12-hour turnaround is now closer to 36 hours, sometimes even 48 hours.

355
00:25:29,060 --> 00:25:33,440
And in global supply chains, that delay accumulates quickly.

356
00:25:33,440 --> 00:25:35,630
It becomes a bottleneck.

357
00:25:35,630 --> 00:25:40,760
Have we considered conducting an operational assessment on their planning capabilities?

358
00:25:40,760 --> 00:25:42,640
I have, yes.

359
00:25:42,640 --> 00:25:47,840
In fact, I drafted a preliminary vendor capability assessment framework last week.

360
00:25:47,840 --> 00:25:54,520
It includes criteria like digital maturity, demand, planning responsiveness, risk management

361
00:25:54,520 --> 00:25:57,860
structure, and supply chain visibility tools.

362
00:25:57,860 --> 00:26:04,560
But before we implement it, we need internal alignment, especially with legal and compliance.

363
00:26:04,560 --> 00:26:10,460
If we start evaluating suppliers more strictly, some may push back or feel threatened.

364
00:26:10,460 --> 00:26:12,580
But I believe it's necessary.

365
00:26:12,580 --> 00:26:13,860
Definitely necessary.

366
00:26:13,860 --> 00:26:19,020
And honestly, if a supplier feels threatened by transparency and capability assessments,

367
00:26:19,020 --> 00:26:21,220
that might be revealing in itself.

368
00:26:21,220 --> 00:26:25,020
In modern procurement, suppliers should expect evaluation.

369
00:26:25,020 --> 00:26:27,370
It's part of strategic sourcing.

370
00:26:27,370 --> 00:26:31,930
Speaking of strategic sourcing, have you given any more thought to whether we should diversify

371
00:26:31,930 --> 00:26:33,530
our supplier base?

372
00:26:33,530 --> 00:26:39,850
Right now, we rely heavily on three primary suppliers for nearly 80% of our raw material

373
00:26:39,850 --> 00:26:40,850
inputs.

374
00:26:40,850 --> 00:26:42,610
That feels risky.

375
00:26:42,610 --> 00:26:44,050
Very risky.

376
00:26:44,050 --> 00:26:47,610
And the current environment makes it riskier.

377
00:26:47,610 --> 00:26:52,490
Single source or limited source strategies were fine when stability and predictability

378
00:26:52,490 --> 00:26:55,450
were high, but the world has changed.

379
00:26:55,450 --> 00:27:01,930
I actually think supplier diversification is not just an option, it's essential.

380
00:27:01,930 --> 00:27:04,810
The challenge of course is qualifying new suppliers.

381
00:27:04,810 --> 00:27:09,930
The onboarding process is long, but at the same time, it might be longer term pain for

382
00:27:09,930 --> 00:27:11,610
shorter term gain.

383
00:27:11,610 --> 00:27:12,850
Agreed.

384
00:27:12,850 --> 00:27:15,130
And something else has been bothering me.

385
00:27:15,130 --> 00:27:19,530
It's the lack of real-time visibility across our supply chain.

386
00:27:19,530 --> 00:27:22,690
We track shipments, but the data is fragmented.

387
00:27:22,690 --> 00:27:27,930
Our ERP shows one thing, the freight forwarder system shows something else, and sometimes

388
00:27:27,930 --> 00:27:31,320
we don't reconcile the data until days later.

389
00:27:31,320 --> 00:27:35,600
If we're serious about performance improvement, we need end-to-end visibility.

390
00:27:35,600 --> 00:27:36,660
Yes.

391
00:27:36,660 --> 00:27:38,580
Good morning.

392
00:27:38,580 --> 00:27:41,180
I hope you had a smooth commute today.

393
00:27:41,180 --> 00:27:45,940
I know the traffic around the industrial zone has been terrible this week.

394
00:27:45,940 --> 00:27:46,940
Morning.

395
00:27:46,940 --> 00:27:50,020
Yes, it took me a little longer than usual, but it's all good.

396
00:27:50,020 --> 00:27:52,500
I grabbed a coffee on the way to stay energized.

397
00:27:52,500 --> 00:27:56,300
I know we have a long discussion ahead regarding the supply chain bottlenecks and procurement

398
00:27:56,300 --> 00:27:58,220
strategy for the next quarter.

399
00:27:58,220 --> 00:27:59,380
Absolutely.

400
00:27:59,380 --> 00:28:03,060
That's precisely why I wanted us to sit together today.

401
00:28:03,060 --> 00:28:08,540
A lot has changed over the past few weeks, especially with the fluctuations in lead times

402
00:28:08,540 --> 00:28:13,140
and the unexpected delays from two of our main suppliers.

403
00:28:13,140 --> 00:28:17,860
Procurement has been stretched thin and supply chain operations have been reacting

404
00:28:17,860 --> 00:28:19,420
rather than planning.

405
00:28:19,420 --> 00:28:22,780
I believe this is the right moment to align ourselves.

406
00:28:22,780 --> 00:28:23,780
I agree.

407
00:28:23,780 --> 00:28:25,980
In fact, I've been reviewing all the data,

408
00:28:25,980 --> 00:28:28,460
and the patterns are quite concerning.

409
00:28:28,460 --> 00:28:31,260
Supplier A in Malaysia has pushed their lead time

410
00:28:31,260 --> 00:28:34,460
from 22 days to nearly 36 days.

411
00:28:34,460 --> 00:28:36,900
Supplier B in India has been struggling

412
00:28:36,900 --> 00:28:38,260
with raw material shortages,

413
00:28:38,260 --> 00:28:41,340
which is affecting their ability to dispatch on time.

414
00:28:41,340 --> 00:28:43,380
And Supplier C from Vietnam

415
00:28:43,380 --> 00:28:45,500
has introduced a new quality inspection cycle

416
00:28:45,500 --> 00:28:48,420
that adds an additional five days to their timeline.

417
00:28:48,420 --> 00:28:51,860
these shifts are creating ripple effects across the entire chain.

418
00:28:51,860 --> 00:28:58,340
Exactly. And what's even more worrying is the impact this is having on our production planning.

419
00:28:58,340 --> 00:29:03,940
When inbound materials don't arrive consistently, the factory floor ends up either

420
00:29:03,940 --> 00:29:10,500
overstocked or undersupplied. Last week, production had to slow down because one critical

421
00:29:10,500 --> 00:29:16,020
component wasn't available, even though inventories of other components were piling up.

422
00:29:16,020 --> 00:29:18,840
that imbalance is costing us.

423
00:29:18,840 --> 00:29:21,540
Yes, and that's why I think we need to rethink

424
00:29:21,540 --> 00:29:24,740
how procurement interacts with supply chain operations.

425
00:29:24,740 --> 00:29:27,180
Right now, we're functioning too independently.

426
00:29:27,180 --> 00:29:30,380
Procurement is negotiating contracts and placing orders

427
00:29:30,380 --> 00:29:32,220
while supply chain is tracking deliveries

428
00:29:32,220 --> 00:29:33,900
and planning logistics.

429
00:29:33,900 --> 00:29:36,340
But the two processes need to be more integrated.

430
00:29:36,340 --> 00:29:39,060
Otherwise, we will continue to experience these gaps.

431
00:29:39,060 --> 00:29:39,980
You're right.

432
00:29:39,980 --> 00:29:43,420
It's no longer enough to just source cost effectively,

433
00:29:43,420 --> 00:29:45,780
we have to source reliably.

434
00:29:45,780 --> 00:29:52,100
Supplier performance should be evaluated not only based on price and quality, but also consistency,

435
00:29:52,100 --> 00:29:57,780
responsiveness, communication speed, and their ability to handle fluctuations.

436
00:29:57,780 --> 00:30:02,500
Right now, our vendor scorecards only capture part of the picture.

437
00:30:02,500 --> 00:30:06,740
That's one of the things I wanted us to address. For example, Supplier A has good pricing,

438
00:30:06,740 --> 00:30:10,980
but their communication is extremely slow. Sometimes it takes them two or three days

439
00:30:10,980 --> 00:30:16,740
just to confirm a dispatch date. When unexpected delays occur, they notify us too late. That

440
00:30:16,740 --> 00:30:19,900
lack of transparency hurts our planning accuracy.

441
00:30:19,900 --> 00:30:25,820
Precisely. And I've noticed that when we follow up with them repeatedly, we still don't get

442
00:30:25,820 --> 00:30:31,820
proactive responses. They still wait until we push them. This reactive communication

443
00:30:31,820 --> 00:30:36,860
style does not work for a high volume production environment like ours.

444
00:30:36,860 --> 00:30:41,540
So one thing we could do is implement a more structured communication protocol.

445
00:30:41,540 --> 00:30:46,300
We could require weekly updates from all suppliers, short bulletins stating their raw material

446
00:30:46,300 --> 00:30:50,500
situation, production progress, and expected dispatch schedules.

447
00:30:50,500 --> 00:30:53,900
This way we get early warning signals instead of last-minute surprises.

448
00:30:53,900 --> 00:30:55,460
I like that approach.

449
00:30:55,460 --> 00:31:00,380
It's simple, sustainable, and doesn't require any major investment.

450
00:31:00,380 --> 00:31:04,620
And more importantly, it sets a clear expectation.

451
00:31:04,620 --> 00:31:08,060
will know that silence is not acceptable.

452
00:31:08,060 --> 00:31:13,740
Yes. Another topic I wanted to address is safety stock. I know maintaining too much inventory

453
00:31:13,740 --> 00:31:18,260
increases our carrying costs, but with the current volatility, I believe we need to review

454
00:31:18,260 --> 00:31:23,780
our safety stock levels. Some items may require temporary increases until the supplier situation

455
00:31:23,780 --> 00:31:24,780
stabilizes.

456
00:31:24,780 --> 00:31:30,680
That's a good point. Historically, our safety stock was calculated based on average

457
00:31:30,680 --> 00:31:37,960
lead times and average consumption, but today the variance is so high that those calculations

458
00:31:37,960 --> 00:31:40,200
don't reflect reality anymore.

459
00:31:40,200 --> 00:31:47,400
If a supplier suddenly extends their lead time by 10 to 14 days, we risk stock outs.

460
00:31:47,400 --> 00:31:48,400
Exactly.

461
00:31:48,400 --> 00:31:50,960
We cannot rely on outdated parameters.

462
00:31:50,960 --> 00:31:56,000
I think we should recalculate safety stock using higher variability assumptions.

463
00:31:56,000 --> 00:31:59,920
Perhaps we could also use a dynamic safety stock model that adjusts based on real-time

464
00:31:59,920 --> 00:32:06,080
demand and supplier performance. That would be ideal. It may require support from the planning

465
00:32:06,080 --> 00:32:13,040
team, but it's doable. If we present a clear case showing how recent delays have affected production,

466
00:32:13,040 --> 00:32:18,400
I'm sure we can get quick approval. Absolutely. Speaking of planning, I think we need to

467
00:32:18,400 --> 00:32:23,120
strengthen the relationship between procurement, supply chain, and production planning.

468
00:32:23,920 --> 00:32:28,720
Right now, each team has their own set of priorities and sometimes communication becomes

469
00:32:28,720 --> 00:32:34,400
fragmented. True. I've seen situations where planning has changed production

470
00:32:34,400 --> 00:32:38,960
schedules without informing procurement early enough so we weren't able to

471
00:32:38,960 --> 00:32:43,920
adjust purchase orders in time. That creates unnecessary pressure on both

472
00:32:43,920 --> 00:32:48,800
sides. And the opposite happens as well. Procurement sometimes changes order

473
00:32:48,800 --> 00:32:52,760
quantities or delivery schedules based on negotiations with suppliers but

474
00:32:52,760 --> 00:32:56,520
planning doesn't get updated on time. This creates confusion on the shop

475
00:32:56,520 --> 00:32:58,920
flooring forces last-minute adjustments.

476
00:32:58,920 --> 00:33:01,500
All of this highlights one thing.

477
00:33:01,500 --> 00:33:03,920
We need improved alignment.

478
00:33:03,920 --> 00:33:09,200
And not just occasional meetings, we need a structured process.

479
00:33:09,200 --> 00:33:10,200
Good morning.

